# The Bloom Surface already has the coordinate split needed for a safe
# lower-resolution auxiliary target.  Its requested dimensions drive the GL
# viewport and texture UV extent, while Game::Render activates it with the
# `screen-space` flag: KAGE::UpdateProjection then deliberately uses the global
# 480x270 virtual extent instead of the Surface dimensions.  The composite
# renders that texture into an independently constructed 480x270 quad and gets
# its texel step from the padded backing dimensions.  With the accepted KAGE
# align8 policy, the early exact 480x270 creation is already half of the Vita
# logical scanout and gets a 480x272 backing, so it must remain untouched.
# KAGE later publishes its fixed 960x540 framebuffer while retaining the
# game's 480x270 virtual projection, then asks Game::Render to recreate these
# targets.  Only that exact 960x540 Bloom recreation becomes 480x270 with the
# same 480x272 backing.  The auxiliary target therefore stays stable across
# the lifecycle without changing the 960x540 main render or scene/UI
# coordinates.  This remains a visible quality tradeoff, not pixel
# equivalence.
VITA_TRANSIENT_BLOOM_HALF_RES_CREATE_ROOT_RVA = 0x002C7E70
